<dialog id="physics-notes" aria-labelledby="physics-notes-title">
    <form method="dialog"><button class="close-dialog" aria-label="Close research notes">Close <span aria-hidden="true">×</span></button></form>
    <p class="project-context">Physics-informed neural networks</p>
<h2 id="physics-notes-title">Working backward<br>from noisy prices</h2>
    <p>The forward problem takes a volatility surface and produces prices. The inverse problem tries to recover that surface from observed prices. Noise makes the second problem much harder.</p>
    <p>I implemented inverse PINN and neural-adjoint solvers using Black–Scholes and Dupire constraints, and compared error, smoothness, training time, and stability at 0%, 1%, and 5% synthetic noise.</p>
    <p>The result is a 29-page research paper. These are controlled synthetic experiments; the work doesn't establish live-market calibration performance.</p>
    <a class="underlined" href="/Adam_Thorne_PINN_Paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the paper ↗︎</a>
  </dialog>
<dialog id="forecast-notes" aria-labelledby="forecast-notes-title">
    <form method="dialog"><button class="close-dialog" aria-label="Close forecasting notes">Close <span aria-hidden="true">×</span></button></form>
    <p class="project-context">Multimodal corn futures forecasting</p>
<h2 id="forecast-notes-title">Putting the signals<br>together</h2>
    <p>ResNet-50 produces embeddings from satellite images. I fused those representations with weather, seasonality, and futures prices in an LSTM to forecast corn price changes.</p>
    <p>I used chronological splits and fit normalization on the training data only, keeping later observations out of the training setup.</p>
  </dialog>
